1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,323
A deterministic feature can afford to instrument late.

2
00:00:03,323 --> 00:00:07,782
The button did the same thing in January that it does today, so a metric added in

3
00:00:07,782 --> 00:00:11,526
March can still be reasoned about honestly for the months before it.

4
00:00:11,526 --> 00:00:13,948
An AI feature doesn't get that grace period.

5
00:00:13,948 --> 00:00:18,297
The exact model, the exact prompt version, the exact retrieved context behind a

6
00:00:18,297 --> 00:00:22,922
specific answer exists for one moment, the moment it's generated, and then it's gone

7
00:00:22,922 --> 00:00:24,518
unless something captured it.

8
00:00:24,518 --> 00:00:29,088
Add acceptance tracking three months after launch and you haven't just missed three

9
00:00:29,088 --> 00:00:30,134
months of a number.

10
00:00:30,134 --> 00:00:34,648
You've lost the ability to ever know what those three months actually looked like.

11
00:00:34,648 --> 00:00:38,998
That's the entire argument for this lecture existing before any other analytics

12
00:00:38,998 --> 00:00:43,347
work: instrumentation is the one part of this section that has to happen first,

13
00:00:43,347 --> 00:00:46,044
because it can't be reconstructed after the fact.

14
00:00:46,044 --> 00:00:50,669
This is worth internalising before the engineering conversation even starts, because

15
00:00:50,669 --> 00:00:54,688
instrumentation usually gets scoped by an engineer working alone, without

16
00:00:54,688 --> 00:00:58,376
necessarily knowing which numbers this section is about to ask for.

17
00:00:58,376 --> 00:01:02,836
Bring this schema to that conversation yourself, before the sprint that ships the

18
00:01:02,836 --> 00:01:04,487
feature, not the sprint after.

19
00:01:05,037 --> 00:01:10,053
Read the request_id column down the table, because it's doing the actual work here.

20
00:01:10,053 --> 00:01:14,888
Every one of these four events shares the same identifier, so any outcome traces

21
00:01:14,888 --> 00:01:19,843
back to the exact shown event that produced it, and from there to the exact prompt

22
00:01:19,843 --> 00:01:22,019
version and model that generated it.

23
00:01:22,019 --> 00:01:26,612
This is the same discipline 7.2 already asked of your cost model: every call

24
00:01:26,612 --> 00:01:31,688
identifiable, every number traceable to the request that produced it, not aggregated

25
00:01:31,688 --> 00:01:33,139
into an anonymous total.

26
00:01:33,139 --> 00:01:37,792
If you're already logging a request_id for cost, this schema mostly extends a

27
00:01:37,792 --> 00:01:39,484
pattern that already exists.

28
00:01:39,484 --> 00:01:44,137
Notice the feedback row is honestly the weakest of the four, and that's fine.

29
00:01:44,137 --> 00:01:46,615
Most users never click thumbs up or down.

30
00:01:46,615 --> 00:01:51,389
Still worth capturing: on the rare request where someone does, it's the closest

31
00:01:51,389 --> 00:01:54,411
thing to direct ground truth this schema produces.

32
00:01:54,411 --> 00:01:59,366
The escalation row deserves its own moment: it's the one most likely to be missing

33
00:01:59,366 --> 00:02:02,992
on a feature that wasn't built with a human handoff in mind.

34
00:02:02,992 --> 00:02:05,349
Take the refund agent from Section six.

35
00:02:05,349 --> 00:02:07,887
Does it hand a case like 4471 to a person?

36
00:02:07,887 --> 00:02:12,843
That's exactly what 9.1's deflection metric needs, tagged with the same request_id

37
00:02:12,843 --> 00:02:17,557
as everything else, not reconstructed later from a support ticket system never

38
00:02:17,557 --> 00:02:19,430
designed to connect back to it.

39
00:02:19,980 --> 00:02:23,332
Notice the bad column isn't a story about lazy engineering.

40
00:02:23,332 --> 00:02:27,537
It's what happens by default when instrumentation gets treated as a launch

41
00:02:27,537 --> 00:02:32,253
nice-to-have instead of a launch requirement, the same way a golden set got treated

42
00:02:32,253 --> 00:02:35,265
as optional before Section four made the case for it.

43
00:02:35,265 --> 00:02:39,754
The good column's real payoff shows up the first time you ship a prompt change.

44
00:02:39,754 --> 00:02:44,470
With a request_id tying every outcome to a version, acceptance before and after the

45
00:02:44,470 --> 00:02:47,481
change is two clean numbers you can compare directly.

46
00:02:47,481 --> 00:02:52,198
Without it, you're left asking everyone in the room whether the new version "feels"

47
00:02:52,198 --> 00:02:56,857
better, which is exactly the kind of guess this whole course has been teaching you

48
00:02:56,857 --> 00:02:57,482
to replace.

49
00:02:57,482 --> 00:03:00,437
Run the maths on what the bad column actually costs.

50
00:03:00,437 --> 00:03:05,039
Three months of real usage, at whatever volume your feature actually sees, is the

51
00:03:05,039 --> 00:03:09,358
single richest dataset you'll ever have about how real users respond to real

52
00:03:09,358 --> 00:03:09,812
outputs.

53
00:03:09,812 --> 00:03:13,051
Losing it to a missing identifier isn't a rounding error.

54
00:03:13,051 --> 00:03:17,597
It's the exact evidence a redesign conversation six months from now will wish it

55
00:03:17,597 --> 00:03:17,824
had.

56
00:03:18,374 --> 00:03:23,009
Say the honest caveat, because "log everything, decide what matters later" sounds

57
00:03:23,009 --> 00:03:27,415
thorough and usually produces the opposite: a mass of events nobody can query

58
00:03:27,415 --> 00:03:31,478
cleanly, with the four that actually matter buried somewhere inside it.

59
00:03:31,478 --> 00:03:33,596
Start from the metric, not the event.

60
00:03:33,596 --> 00:03:38,231
Adoption, acceptance, and deflection each need a specific, small set of fields to

61
00:03:38,231 --> 00:03:38,689
compute.

62
00:03:38,689 --> 00:03:43,267
Build to that list deliberately, and add more only when a real question needs an

63
00:03:43,267 --> 00:03:45,327
answer this schema doesn't have yet.

64
00:03:45,327 --> 00:03:49,504
This also has to run through 8.3's questions before a single event ships.

65
00:03:49,504 --> 00:03:54,197
Whatever you log about a request is data you now hold, subject to the same PII and

66
00:03:54,197 --> 00:03:57,687
residency questions as anything else this course has covered.

67
00:03:57,687 --> 00:03:59,690
Log the request_id and the outcome.

68
00:03:59,690 --> 00:04:03,581
Think hard before logging the full text of every request by default.

69
00:04:03,581 --> 00:04:08,102
This is the same discipline as 9.1's acceptance metric turned inward on itself:

70
00:04:08,102 --> 00:04:12,737
define the number first, then build only what computes it, rather than collecting

71
00:04:12,737 --> 00:04:16,056
broadly and hoping a clean metric falls out the other end.

72
00:04:16,056 --> 00:04:16,915
It rarely does.

73
00:04:16,915 --> 00:04:21,493
A broad collection effort with no metric driving it tends to produce exactly the

74
00:04:21,493 --> 00:04:24,239
unqueryable pile this caveat is warning against.

75
00:04:24,789 --> 00:04:29,344
That's the floor worth building before launch, not after, and the example is worth

76
00:04:29,344 --> 00:04:32,898
running literally, on a real request, not as a thought exercise.

77
00:04:32,898 --> 00:04:37,397
Most teams running this for the first time discover the honest answer is no, they

78
00:04:37,397 --> 00:04:41,730
can't reconstruct last week's request, because the request_id thread was never

79
00:04:41,730 --> 00:04:43,396
actually connected end to end.

80
00:04:43,396 --> 00:04:48,006
That gap is exactly what this lecture exists to close, before it costs three months

81
00:04:48,006 --> 00:04:49,117
instead of one week.

82
00:04:49,117 --> 00:04:52,227
Widen the exercise if the first attempt succeeds easily.

83
00:04:52,227 --> 00:04:55,115
Pick a request from a month ago, then a quarter ago.

84
00:04:55,115 --> 00:04:59,669
The point where the honest answer turns from yes to no is worth knowing precisely,

85
00:04:59,669 --> 00:05:04,224
because that's the exact date this schema needs to have been live by, and probably

86
00:05:04,224 --> 00:05:04,613
wasn't.

87
00:05:05,163 --> 00:05:06,817
One thing before you move on.

88
00:05:06,817 --> 00:05:08,756
Check the request_id thread first.

89
00:05:08,756 --> 00:05:13,432
If shown and outcome events exist but don't share an identifier, that's the single

90
00:05:13,432 --> 00:05:16,341
highest-leverage fix available to you this section.

91
00:05:16,341 --> 00:05:20,960
Then confirm, honestly, that what you're logging has already been checked against

92
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:22,215
8.3's three questions.

93
00:05:22,215 --> 00:05:24,895
Neither step needs new infrastructure to start.

94
00:05:24,895 --> 00:05:29,628
The request_id check is a five-minute look at your existing event logs, and the 8.3

95
00:05:29,628 --> 00:05:34,191
check is a conversation you've already had once this course, applied to one more

96
00:05:34,191 --> 00:05:34,590
system.

97
00:05:34,590 --> 00:05:39,210
Next lecture is A/B testing a feature that gives a different answer every time it

98
00:05:39,210 --> 00:05:44,000
runs, and why the standard test you already know needs one real adjustment before it

99
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:45,882
works on something probabilistic.
